{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm trying to demonstrate a deliberately stale `state.json` snapshot, but the current setup shows the live and snapshot states as equal for the harness UI. To honestly show staleness, I need a fixture project where `state.json` differs from the live state. I'll create a fixture under `version 0.1.0.001/fixtures/` with two mini-projects: one missing `state.json` and another with stale snapshot IDs. This will allow deterministic enactment of absence, staleness, and liveness age."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:02.400080+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nTesting the stale refresh mechanism in headless mode is tricky because the dump-dom command exits immediately. Using Chrome's `--virtual-time-budget` flag seems promising, as it allows timers to fire rapidly, simulating a longer runtime. I can then instrument the page to check for a refresh status marker, like a `data-polls` attribute, to confirm the mechanism works. For liveness and honest absence, fixture runs with controlled file modification times and missing state files should suffice."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:06.319222300+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI need to ensure fixture projects under `version 0.1.0.001/fixtures/` are correctly discovered by the emitter when run with `-Root fixtures`, which requires them to have `.devin/` and `devlog.md`. The harness-ui itself will be discovered correctly in the real run. I'm now focusing on writing the emitter script for PowerShell 5.1, paying close attention to the `devlog.md` structure, including its sections, cycle entries, and the detailed bullet points within each entry, especially the multi-line `Result:` and the final `Verdict:` followed by JSON blocks."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:09.962670+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm parsing the devlog structure, identifying sections and entries by their `##` and `###` headings. JSON blocks are associated with the preceding entry, and their \"kind\" is determined by the last `**X verdict:**` marker. I'm extracting bullet points and multi-line continuations using regex. Timestamps are parsed from `### Cycle` headings and `**Time to implement:**` lines. Build folders are associated with cycles by version regex or fallback to entry order. Convention IDs are collected from multiple sources, and cell statuses are derived from disposition text in FEATURES.md."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:13.127678+00:00"}}}}
