{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I've noticed a discrepancy between the corpus prompt file's modification date and the snapshot date, indicating potential revision drift. Since the live prompt lacks an explicit revision marker, I'll use the file's modification time as a proxy, clearly labeling it as such. Now, I'm setting up the build structure and will begin with the emitter script, ensuring it's compatible with PS 5.1."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:27.021701100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm wrestling with how to implement the `-Once` and `-Watch` flags in PowerShell. The spec implies `-Watch` takes an argument for seconds, defaulting to 30, but PowerShell's parameter binding makes it tricky to handle a bare `-Watch` versus `-Watch 30`. I'm considering using a `[switch]$Watch` and `[int]$Interval = 30` as a cleaner approach, even if it slightly deviates from the literal spec wording."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:29.346401500+00:00"}}}}
