{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I've hit a StrictMode error because I'm trying to access properties that might not exist on `PSCustomObject`s, like `$sp.verdict.conventions`. Under StrictMode 2.0, this throws a `PropertyNotFoundStrict` error. I need to add safe access checks, like using `$sp.verdict.PSObject.Properties['conventions']` or checking if the property exists, for many places where I dynamically access properties. I'll write a helper function, `GP`, to handle this safely."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:34:05.316355400+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm refining the `GP` helper function for safe property access. It seems I need to use it for `$sp.verdict.conventions`, `$cc.id`, `$sc.id`, `$lastVerdict.verdict.overall`, `$lastVerdict.verdict.judge_model`, `$data.state.line`, `$state.revisions`, and `$sp.verdict.overall`. I also need to apply it to nested properties like `$state.direction_in_force.budget`. Some existing direct property accesses like `$data.status_files` and `$bdir.Name` appear to be fine as they are."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:34:08.120446300+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm re-evaluating my use of `$data.state` and `$data.last_verdict` within Invoke-Scan. While I initially assumed direct property access would fail under StrictMode for ordered dictionaries, PowerShell's adaptation of `IDictionary` allows property-style access (`$od.key`) which returns `$null` for missing keys, unlike true `.NET` property access. The actual error occurred with `$sp.verdict.conventions` on a `PSCustomObject`, which lacks that property entirely. I need to use `GP` for `PSCustomObject` property access, but ordered dictionary access like `$data.series` seems safe."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:34:09.795658700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm finding that `$sp.verdict` is an `[ordered]@{}` but its properties like `.conventions` are `PSCustomObject`s, causing strict errors. I need to use `GP` for all `PSCustomObject` property access, including nested ones like `(GP (GP $data.state 'direction_in_force') 'ref')`. Ordered dictionaries like `$data.state` and `$e.bullets` are fine for direct property access, as are hashtables like `$s` in `foreach ($s in $suite)`."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:34:11.894829100+00:00"}}}}
