{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe `ConvertTo-Json` cmdlet's performance issues might stem from its handling of `OrderedDictionary` types and deep nesting, even if the object graph isn't that deep. The large `notes` strings within the `verdict` object contribute to the overall size, but the minutes-long execution time suggests a more fundamental problem with `ConvertTo-Json`'s reflection-based approach on complex objects. Reducing the depth to 12 is a pragmatic test, but the core issue might be the cmdlet's inherent inefficiency."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:37:47.323308100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm checking if `Write-Host` is the bottleneck for `data.js` generation, but `Write-TextFile` uses `WriteAllText`, so that's unlikely. Instead, I'll optimize by emitting the raw verdict JSON directly into the payload. This preserves fidelity and avoids re-serialization issues like lost key order and formatting, which is more honest regardless of speed."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:37:47.928064200+00:00"}}}}
