{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"It seems the `ConvertTo-Json -Depth 40` call is the bottleneck, likely due to quadratic behavior with OrderedDictionaries in PowerShell 5.1. Instead of re-serializing, I'll manually assemble `data.js`, converting smaller parts to JSON at a lower depth and embedding raw JSON strings for the verdict data."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:39:27.173735600+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm considering a simpler, more robust approach: build the entire data object with verdicts as placeholder strings, serialize it to JSON, and then use string replacement to insert the raw JSON verdicts. This keeps the `verdict` parsed in the live object for internal use while ensuring correct serialization. I'll also reduce the JSON depth to 12 and add a timebox for safety. The `$state` PSCustomObject might also be a culprit due to its nested structure."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:39:29.454305100+00:00"}}}}
