{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBuilding series items with a placeholder like `'@@V#@@'` directly seems simpler than a deep copy, but it complicates retrieving the overall verdict. Storing the raw verdict alongside the placeholder in the OrderedDictionary would duplicate large strings, doubling the output size. Instead, I'll store the raw verdict separately and replace the placeholder with a reference to it during serialization, then remove the raw data before emitting."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:39:32.612065100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm serializing the payload to JSON and then replacing placeholder strings like `\"@@V1@@\"` with the raw JSON verdicts. String replacement is efficient, but I need to be careful that the placeholder in the JSON is quoted, so the replacement must include the quotes. OrderedDictionary's `Remove` method works fine, and assigning to `$sp.verdict` also functions as expected. For unparsed verdicts, `$sp.verdict` remains null, which is handled correctly by the JavaScript checks, though it means the raw text is lost. I could include `verdict_raw` as a plain string field for display, but that would increase data size, so I'll stick to using it only for placeholders."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:39:35.177797100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm verifying that `ConvertTo-Json` in PowerShell 5.1 correctly serializes `OrderedDictionary` objects. It appears to treat `IDictionary` specially, serializing them as objects with ordered key-value pairs, which is what I need. I also need to add the `raw` field to the verdict record creation. The placeholder approach for verdicts should be faster than re-serializing large JSON payloads, avoiding the known ~60-120 second slowness of `ConvertTo-Json` on large nested objects."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:39:36.043550700+00:00"}}}}
