{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"The serialization isn't the bottleneck; depth 15 is still slow. The `ConvertTo-Json` of the `[ordered]` dictionary might be the issue, though known performance problems with many properties don't fully explain the 5+ minute delay. I'm checking if the slowness is actually within the corpus extraction itself, or potentially `Get-Content` calls."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:42:07.235833500+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm now suspecting `ConvertTo-Json` itself is the bottleneck, not serialization depth. PowerShell 5.1's `ConvertTo-Json` appears to have a severe performance issue with `[ordered]` dictionaries, especially with large payloads, beyond the known `-Compress` problem. The `Parse-Devlog` and merge loops seem too fast to be the cause."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:42:10.258881200+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe serialization of the `[ordered]` dictionary itself seems fast enough, with verdicts as short strings and other large components totaling only about 15KB. This suggests the bottleneck isn't the final serialization but must occur earlier, possibly within the `Build-ProjectData` function after the corpus log, or perhaps in the `Invoke-Scan` function's steps like `ConvertTo-Json` or file operations."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:42:13.361071100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm re-evaluating the `[ordered]` dictionary handling in PowerShell 5.1. While `Remove` and property-style indexer setting (`$sp.verdict = ...`) appear to work correctly for `OrderedDictionary`, the `ConvertTo-Json` operation on the ~15-20KB payload remains the primary suspect for performance issues, unless there's a pathological object graph I've missed. I'm now checking CPU usage and output file timestamps to see if the process actually completed."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:42:14.511224900+00:00"}}}}
