{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"The massive memory usage during serialization suggests infinite recursion or exponential expansion, likely within `ConvertTo-Json`. While I initially suspected `OrderedDictionary`, the issue might be the data *within* its properties, such as `$d.status_files` or `$d.suite` entries. The placeholder loop is also a candidate for the memory growth, specifically the `$sp.verdict='@@VERDICT_'+$rawList.Count+'@@'` assignment."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:45:06.144182800+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait, `return [ordered]@{...}` in PowerShell enumerates the OrderedDictionary into DictionaryEntry objects. So `$d` becomes an array of DictionaryEntry, not the dictionary itself. Accessing `$d.series` on this array would fail because DictionaryEntry has no 'series' property. This explains why it worked earlier: the harness-ui's Build-ProjectData also returned `[ordered]`, but perhaps it didn't error out due to PowerShell's member enumeration behavior or strict mode not being fully enforced."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:45:08.347932700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm reconsidering PowerShell's output enumeration. While functions typically unroll collections, `OrderedDictionary` and `Hashtable` might be treated as single objects, especially when passed to `ConvertTo-Json`. This aligns with `Write-Output` not enumerating `IDictionary`. The massive size could stem from `ConvertTo-Json`'s bug with nested `OrderedDictionary` and other collection types in PS5.1, though my earlier test with `[ordered]` was fast. The `convention_ids` array, sorted, is another potential candidate."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:45:09.822096600+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'll try serializing each top-level key individually to pinpoint the exact source of the serialization issue. This incremental approach should help isolate the problematic data structure or property causing the excessive memory consumption."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:45:10.220342600+00:00"}}}}
