{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I'm stuck trying to figure out why the FLEET stage is taking so long. It's not the JSON conversion or the per-project mutation loop. I thought it might be the project's JSON payload, but that file was already written. The fleet serialization and file writes seem trivial, and the `Get-ChildItem` call was skipped. I'm not seeing any obvious bottlenecks in the fleet build process itself."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:47:24.682614300+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm reconsidering the `Write-Host` output blocking theory. While the process *is* CPU-bound and not blocked, the `Write-Host` calls within the loop could still be the culprit if the output pipe isn't being drained. However, the `ConvertTo-JsonText` recursion into `status_files` or `state` seems unlikely, as the project payload serialized correctly. I'm now looking at potential infinite recursion within `ConvertTo-JsonText` itself, specifically in the `[regex]::Replace` callback, though the fleet strings are small."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:47:28.293363100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nCPU 77s on trivial work suggest an unexpected process bottleneck. It's not `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` in liveness, as that's per-project. `Save-Log` writing to `evidence/scan-*.log` also seems fine, as the directory exists and the write operation is valid. The fastest way to diagnose this is to add stage logging around the `Invoke-Scan` call and rerun."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:47:28.879465400+00:00"}}}}
