{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I'm seeing an issue where the corpus is reported as \"none derivable\" for `stalerig`, even though the path `C:\\builds\\...\\corpus\\spec\\Prompt.md` appears to match my regex. The regex, `'([A-Za-z]:\\\\[^\\s``\"'']+?\\.(?:md|txt))'`, is enclosed in single quotes, which might be causing issues with escaping the backtick character. I need to re-evaluate how the regex is being interpreted within the single-quoted string."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:52:55.696554100+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait — is the issue the single quote in the file path regex? The character class `[^\\s``\"'']` in the FILE regex `'([A-Za-z]:\\\\[^\\s``\"'']+?\\.(?:md|txt))'` seems correct for PowerShell's single-quoted strings. Hmm, but maybe the issue is different: the regex matched, but the ancestor walk looked for `CONVENTIONS.md` in `corpus\\spec` instead of `corpus\\fixtures`. This implies the regex didn't match at all, causing `corpusFile` to be null. Why? The `Get-Content -Raw` of `prompt.md` includes an em-dash `—` after `.md`, which the character class *should* allow. The lazy quantifier `+?` should stop at `\\.md`, so the capture should end at `Prompt.md`. Maybe `Test-Path` failed because `prompt.md` was written with Python's `\\r\\n` line endings? No matter. I'll test the regex directly in PowerShell."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:52:58.091269100+00:00"}}}}
