{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I've found the source of my heredoc backslash issues: the tool's command transport collapses `\\\\` to `\\` before bash even sees it. This means I need to quadruple backslashes (`\\\\\\\\`) in heredocs to get a literal `\\` in the file, or use character codes. This also explains why the emitter fix failed; the regex in the file *should* have had `\\\\` to match a literal backslash, but it seems to have been collapsed there too."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:53:09.437487700+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWait, in the t5.ps1 test, the pattern `:\\\\` in my heredoc source became `:\\` in the file due to transport collapse. This means the regex `:\\` was interpreted as an escaped colon, not a literal backslash followed by a colon. The error \"Not enough )'s\" suggests the `\\` was then seen as escaping the closing parenthesis of the regex, invalidating the test. The pattern in the emitter file is likely correct, but I need to re-examine its bytes to be sure."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:53:10.194068300+00:00"}}}}
