{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I'm seeing literal `bs` tokens in the PowerShell output, but I expected Python to concatenate the `bs` variable's value. This suggests the Python string itself contains the text `bs`, not a variable. Looking at the code, `'$m3 = [regex]::Match($t, \"([A-Za-z]:' + bs + bs + bs + '\\\\S+)')'`, the `+ bs +` appears to be inside the string literal, not a concatenation. This means I likely made a syntax error with the quotes."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:54:33.025323400+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nAh, I see the issue. The Python string literal starts with `'` and ends with the *next* unescaped `'`. In my line `'$m3 = [regex]::Match($t, \"([A-Za-z]:\" + bs + bs + bs + \"\\\\S+)\")'`, the single quote within `\"\\\\S+'\"` is *not* escaped, and it's not the final quote. This means the entire expression, including `+ bs +`, is treated as literal text within the string. I need to close the string after the colon and then concatenate `bs`."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:54:34.889400700+00:00"}}}}
