{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"I'm writing a render-check script, and I'm considering using PowerShell with Edge headless, but I'm leaning towards a bash script. It'll run Edge headless to dump DOMs for various views and fixtures, then use a Python checker to parse those DOMs and count elements like table rows, SVG circles, and specific CSS classes. Finally, it will write a Markdown file summarizing these counts against the source data."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:55:34.868196400+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nEnacting the stale-refresh scenario headlessly is tricky. My initial plan to use `--virtual-time-budget` to speed up timers doesn't quite work because `generated_at` only updates when the emitter re-runs, not just when the interval fires. A two-step process—dumping the DOM, then updating `data.js` and reloading—might work, but the timing is complex. A simpler approach is to manually stamp `data.js` and reload, which I can test with a script that serves two versions of `data.js` to verify the \"refocus reloads only on newer stamp\" behavior."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:55:37.606405+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm trying to test the stale-refresh scenario headlessly by running Edge with `--virtual-time-budget=60000` on a `file://` page. The idea is that the poll at virtual 30s will pick up a newer `generated_at` timestamp after the emitter re-runs in parallel. This should cause the DOM to re-render with the new stamp, which I can then grep. However, virtual time speeds up timers but not real I/O like script reloads, making the timing tricky. If this fails, I'll try a two-stage check: load initial data, simulate an emitter re-run by overwriting `data.js`, and then dump the DOM again. This would confirm cache-bust reloads but not the refocus trigger, which relies on `visibilitychange`/focus events that are hard to simulate headlessly."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:55:39.328151600+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe virtual-time budget approach for testing stale-refresh is problematic. While it speeds up timers, it doesn't account for the real-time I/O of script reloads. If `data.js` is regenerated before the dump, the timestamp will already be updated, invalidating the test. A better approach might be to ensure `data.js` changes *between* the initial load and the poll, but this race condition is difficult to achieve. Accessing the IIFE internals for a direct poll call is also not feasible."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:55:40.643470900+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"ubiquitous-eyebrow","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe current approach to testing stale-refresh using virtual time is fragile because it doesn't account for real-time I/O like script reloads. A more robust method involves continuously updating a marker field in `data.js` within a virtual-time budget and then verifying that the DOM reflects these changes, proving the poll-reload mechanism works end-to-end even with timing races. I'll implement scripts to dump DOMs and check them, then run the virtual-time test."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:55:42.353361500+00:00"}}}}
